.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt READ 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm read ,
.Nm readv
.Nd read bytes from a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn read "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t count"
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn readv "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn read
reads up to
.Fa count
bytes from
.Fa fd
into
.Fa buf .
A successful call may return fewer bytes than requested.
A zero-length read checks that the descriptor exists, then returns zero
without inspecting the buffer or checking the descriptor's read mode.
.Pp
Regular files are read at the open-file description's offset.
A positive result advances that offset by the number of bytes read.
Descriptors shared through duplication or fork share the offset.
Directories cannot be read with these calls: a nonzero read returns
.Er EBADF .
Use
.Xr getdents64 2
to read directory entries.
.Pp
A pipe read returns the available bytes, up to the requested count.
An empty pipe waits while a writer remains open and returns zero after
the last writer closes.
The console and pseudoterminal slaves use the terminal line discipline:
canonical input waits for a line; raw input follows the
.Dv VMIN
setting.
A pseudoterminal master reads output from the slave and returns zero
after all slaves close and the buffered output is drained.
A slave drains buffered input after its master closes, then returns zero.
.Pp
A background process reading its controlling terminal receives
.Dv SIGTTIN .
If that signal is blocked or ignored, the read fails with
.Er EIO .
When the caller owns the framebuffer, a console read instead returns
raw keyboard scancodes without waiting.
.Pp
A stream socket returns buffered bytes and returns zero after the peer's
FIN once its buffered data is exhausted.
A datagram read consumes one message and discards any bytes beyond the
buffer capacity.
A zero-byte datagram returns zero without indicating end of input.
Protocol-bearing raw sockets can also be read; an ioctl-only socket
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
makes an empty pipe, terminal, pseudoterminal, or socket return
.Er EAGAIN
instead of waiting, unless it is at end of input.
A signal can interrupt a wait with
.Er EINTR .
Files under
.Pa /proc
read from the snapshot made when opened.
Other devices use their own read operation.
Mouse reads return whole packets without waiting and return zero when
the buffer cannot hold one packet.
.Ss Scatter reads
.Fn readv
reads into the
.Fa iovcnt
buffers described by
.Fa iov :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct iovec {
        void *iov_base;
        size_t iov_len;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Buffers are filled in order; zero-length entries are skipped.
The count must be between 0 and 1024, and the sum of the lengths must
not exceed 2147483647.
The kernel checks the whole vector list and write access to every
nonempty destination before taking input.
An invalid destination therefore fails before consuming input.
A list requesting no bytes still checks the descriptor and returns zero
only if it exists.
.Pp
The first nonempty buffer uses an ordinary read, which may wait.
For a regular file, reading continues through successive buffers until
a short read or error.
For a pipe, stream socket, device, or
.Pa /proc
file, later buffers are read only while input can be obtained without waiting.
A terminal is read only once, into the first nonempty buffer.
.Pp
For a
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
socket, exactly one message is taken into a kernel buffer before it is
scattered across the destinations.
Bytes beyond the combined capacity are discarded.
If a destination overwrites the caller's own vector list, later copies
are checked again; a failed copy stops the transfer and returns the
number already copied.
The consumed message is not restored.
.Pp
Musl's buffered input uses
.Fn readv
and handles short returns by reading again.
The i386 system call numbers are 3 for
.Nm read
and 145 for
.Nm readv .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the number of bytes read.
Zero denotes end of a file or stream, a zero-length request, an empty
datagram, or a device's empty result.
.Fn readv
returns the total across the buffers; an error after a positive partial
transfer returns that total.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/readvmusl.c
reads a file containing
.Ql AAAABBBBCCCC
into three four-byte buffers with
.Fn readv .
It requires a return of 12 and the contents
.Ql AAAA ,
.Ql BBBB ,
and
.Ql CCCC
in that order.
It also checks pipe and datagram reads and that an invalid later buffer
leaves input queued.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECONNREFUSED
.It Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking input would have to wait.
.It Er EBADF
The descriptor is invalid, is not open for reading, has no read
operation, or refers to a directory.
.It Er ECONNREFUSED
A stream connection was refused.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A stream connection was reset.
.It Er EFAULT
The buffer or vector list cannot be accessed as required, or there is
no current process.
.It Er EFBIG
An ext2 read requires a block beyond the file system's addressing limit.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a wait, or a background terminal read posted
.Dv SIGTTIN .
.It Er EINVAL
The vector count is outside 0 through 1024, the total vector length
exceeds 2147483647, or a file system has no read operation.
A raw disk window also returns this error when the offset or count is
not a multiple of 512 bytes.
.It Er EIO
A file system or device read failed, a device was removed, or the
random device has no usable seed.
A background terminal read also gives this error when
.Dv SIGTTIN
is blocked or ignored.
.It Er ENETRESET
A stream connection was ended by a network configuration change.
.It Er ENXIO
A raw disk window is not present.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The descriptor is an ioctl-only socket.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A closed stream has no recorded error to report.
.It Er EPERM
A loop device node was read directly.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
A stream connection timed out.
.El
.Pp
A raw disk read also propagates the block device's error when no sector
has yet been returned.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr getdents64 2 ,
.Xr lseek 2 ,
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr write 2
